WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
The productivity evidence, fairly.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:06.403
Here are the main studies in order, with what each design can and can’t show.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:10.672
A controlled lab task (Peng and colleagues, 2023).

4
00:00:10.952 --> 00:00:18.760
Developers were asked to implement an HTTP server in JavaScript; those with GitHub Copilot finished 55.

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.320
8% faster.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.384
Clean randomization, but one small, well-specified, greenfield task with an early autocomplete-style tool.

7
00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:33.083
Field experiments at three companies (Cui and colleagues, published in Management Science).

8
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:50.941
Randomized rollouts of Copilot at Microsoft, Accenture, and a Fortune 100 company, covering 4,867 developers, found about 26% more completed tasks among developers who used it, with larger gains for less experienced developers.

9
00:00:51.221 --> 00:01:01.141
Real work at scale, but “completed tasks” is a coarse output measure, adoption was only about 60% after a year, and the tool predates today’s agents.

10
00:01:01.421 --> 00:01:05.624
METR’s randomized trial (Becker and colleagues, 2025).

11
00:01:05.904 --> 00:01:20.091
Sixteen experienced open-source developers did 246 real tasks in large repositories they knew well, each task randomly allowed or disallowed AI tools (mostly Cursor Pro with Claude 3.

12
00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.395
5 and 3.

13
00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:22.763
7 Sonnet).

14
00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:26.200
With AI allowed, tasks took 19% longer.

15
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:34.992
Before starting, developers expected a 24% speedup; afterwards, they believed AI had sped them up by about 20%.

16
00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:38.707
The authors cautioned against generalizing beyond their setting.

17
00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:42.059
METR’s follow-up (February 2026).

18
00:01:42.339 --> 00:02:05.699
A larger study from August 2025, with 57 developers, 143 repositories, and more than 800 tasks, produced estimates of an 18% speedup for returning developers (95% interval from 38% faster to 9% slower) and 4% for new recruits (15% faster to 9% slower).

19
00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:22.021
METR’s own conclusion is that the data gives an unreliable signal: more developers refused to participate because they didn’t want to work without AI, and 30 to 50% of surveyed developers said they withheld tasks they didn’t want to do without AI,

20
00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:26.077
both of which likely bias the estimate toward smaller speedups.

21
00:02:26.357 --> 00:02:28.256
METR is redesigning the study.

22
00:02:28.536 --> 00:02:31.480
Survey evidence (DORA, 2025).

23
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.517
Google’s DORA research program surveys thousands of technology professionals.

24
00:02:36.797 --> 00:02:48.659
Its central 2025 finding is that AI acts as an amplifier, magnifying an organization’s existing strengths and weaknesses rather than improving delivery on its own.
